<!-- ============================================================
     DcRAT - Operation DragonReturn Detection Rules
     Threat: DcRAT (Dark Crystal RAT) distributed via fake Indian Income Tax
             filing utility; suspected China-nexus threat actor
     Source: The Hacker News / Seqrite Blog, July 2026
             Operation DragonReturn (Seqrite threat intelligence)
     MITRE ATT&CK Techniques:
       T1204.002 - User Execution: Malicious File (TA0002)
       T1027.003 - Obfuscated Files or Information: Steganography (TA0005)
       T1055     - Process Injection (TA0005)
       T1543.003 - Create or Modify System Process: Windows Service (TA0003)
       T1036.005 - Masquerading: Match Legitimate Name or Location (TA0005)
       T1562.001 - Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify Tools / AMSI (TA0005)
       T1071.001 - Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols (TA0011)
     Rule IDs: 104026-104032
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-19

     Threat Actor: Suspected China-nexus (Silver Fox TTP overlap)

     Threat Summary:

     Operation DragonReturn is a multi-stage espionage campaign targeting
     Indian tax professionals (corporate entities, chartered accountants,
     individual taxpayers) exploiting the AY2026-27 ITR filing season.
     Victims are lured via spear-phishing emails impersonating the Indian
     Income Tax Department (first observed 2026-05-18; fully active as of
     2026-06-17). A ZIP archive poses as the Common Offline Utility and
     contains a fake installer.

     Multi-Stage Infection Chain:
       Stage 1: Fake .exe installer drops background.jpg containing an
                LSB-steganographically hidden payload alongside the
                visible installer UI.
       Stage 2: DLL side-loading chain (CameraUtil.dll, VTCP.dll)
                executed to decode and decrypt the hidden payload.
       Stage 3: Decrypted .NET assembly is loaded fully in-memory via
                reflection; AMSI is patched to prevent detection.
       Stage 4: Decrypted assembly targets svchost.exe for process
                injection (CreateToolhelp32Snapshot + VirtualAllocEx +
                WriteProcessMemory + CreateRemoteThread).
       Stage 5: Persistence established via Windows service "MixedSvc"
                masquerading as "Windows Mixed Reality Service".
       Stage 6: Dual payloads beacon outbound - DcRAT over encrypted
                TLS to 223.26.63.40:2671 (ChinaNet ASN with Chinese-
                language management panel) and a Gh0st RAT derivative
                to kkxqbh[.]top:6666.

     Known C2 Infrastructure (Operation DragonReturn, as of 2026-06-17):
       IPs:     223.26.63.40 (DcRAT TLS C2, port 2671; ChinaNet)
       Domains: kkxqbh[.]top (Gh0st RAT C2, port 6666)
                ouewop[.]com (AsyncRAT/Quasar-family, port 6351)
                1kkkkddd[.]com (supplemental C2 / staging)

     Log source prerequisites:
       - Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate)  -> Wazuh SID 61603
         Required for rules 104026, 104027, 104030.
         Sysmon must capture CommandLine, ParentImage, and Image fields.
       - Sysmon EventID 8 (CreateRemoteThread) -> Wazuh SID 61644 + eventID=8
         Required for rule 104028.
         Sysmon must have EventID 8 logging enabled (off by default in
         minimal configs; add to sysmon_config.xml under CreateRemoteThread).
       - Windows Security / System EventID 7045 (new service install) -> Wazuh SID 60103
         Required for rule 104029.
         Enabled by default in all Windows environments with Wazuh agent.
       - Network/firewall logs -> Wazuh SID 103000
         Required for rules 104031 and 104032.
         Source: FortiGate, pfSense, Windows Filtering Platform (WFP), Sysmon EventID 3.

     Severity tiers:
       12 - PowerShell bitmap steganography image processing (suspicious,
            low false-positive rate, used as triage correlator)
       13 - Fake tax installer spawning interpreter child process (high
            confidence; Indian tax software never spawns cmd/powershell)
       13 - AMSI bypass via .NET reflection in PowerShell (near-certain
            malicious; patching AmsiScanBuffer is not a legitimate admin op)
       14 - CreateRemoteThread from non-system process into svchost.exe
            (near-certain process injection; isolate immediately)
       14 - MixedSvc service installed from non-Microsoft path (confirmed
            DcRAT persistence mechanism)
       14 - DcRAT C2 IOC match (single connection to attributed C2 infra)
       15 - Sustained DcRAT C2 beaconing (2+ connections in 120s from same
            source; active RAT implant confirmed; CRITICAL response)

