  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104026: Fake Indian tax installer spawning child interpreter
       Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1204.002 (User Execution: Malicious File)

       Operation DragonReturn distributes a ZIP archive disguised as the
       Indian Income Tax Department's Common Offline Utility for AY2026-27
       ITR filing. The archive contains a .exe installer that presents a
       plausible tax filing UI while silently executing its malicious payload
       chain in the background.

       Legitimate Indian tax filing utilities (ITR Prep Pro, the official
       Income Tax Department offline utility, ClearTax, etc.) do not spawn
       cmd.exe, powershell.exe, wscript.exe, mshta.exe, rundll32.exe, or
       other scripting interpreters as child processes during normal operation.
       Any Indian tax utility process spawning a scripting engine is a
       near-certain indicator that a trojanized variant has been executed.

       The parentImage pattern targets known official and common third-party
       Indian tax utility executable names. Analysts should extend this list
       as the campaign evolves and new installer filenames are observed.

       False positives: extremely rare. Indian tax software that has
       scripting-based update mechanisms (uncommon) may trigger this.
       Validate the parentImage path: legitimate tax utilities install to
       Program Files or user-accessible paths with vendor-signed binaries.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104026" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)(ITRPrepPro|IncomeTax|itr.*offline|ITR_AY|itr.*filing|IncomeTaxReturn|IT_Offline_Utility|income_tax.*util|offline_itr|tax.*utility|itr_prep|e-Filing)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(powershell|pwsh|cmd|wscript|cscript|mshta|rundll32|regsvr32|certutil|bitsadmin)\.exe$</field>
    <description>DcRAT/DragonReturn: Fake Indian tax utility $(win.eventdata.parentImage) spawned child interpreter $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer) as $(win.eventdata.user) [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)] - Operation DragonReturn distributes trojanized ITR filing software; legitimate Indian tax utilities never spawn cmd/PowerShell; high-confidence initial execution indicator (T1204.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>dcrat,dragonreturn,initial_access,execution,fake_installer,india_tax,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104027: PowerShell bitmap steganography payload extraction
       Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1027.003 (Obfuscated Files or Information: Steganography)

       A distinctive feature of Operation DragonReturn is concealing the
       DcRAT payload inside background.jpg using LSB (Least Significant Bit)
       steganography. The installer drops background.jpg into the working
       directory; a subsequent PowerShell stage reads the image using
       System.Drawing.Bitmap, iterates pixel values, extracts LSB-encoded
       bytes, and reconstructs the decrypted .NET assembly or shellcode.

       System.Drawing bitmap operations in a PowerShell process commandLine
       are highly anomalous in enterprise environments. Legitimate PowerShell
       administration scripts almost never perform GetPixel operations or
       load images via System.Drawing.FromFile. The presence of Bitmap/GetPixel
       in a PowerShell commandLine is a reliable steganographic payload
       extraction indicator.

       False positives: rare. Some image processing automation scripts or
       developer tools may invoke .NET imaging APIs from PowerShell. Correlate
       with rule 104026 (fake tax installer parent) or rule 104030 (AMSI
       bypass) on the same host within the preceding 5-10 minutes to elevate
       confidence to a confirmed Operation DragonReturn execution.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104027" level="12">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(powershell|pwsh)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(GetPixel\(|System\.Drawing\.Bitmap|\.FromFile\b.*\.(jpg|jpeg|png|bmp|gif|ico)|FromStream.*[Ii]mage|background\.jpg|\.GetBitmap\()</field>
    <description>DcRAT/DragonReturn: PowerShell bitmap/image API invocation on $(win.eventdata.computer) as $(win.eventdata.user) [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)] - Operation DragonReturn conceals DcRAT payload in background.jpg via LSB steganography; PowerShell using System.Drawing.Bitmap or GetPixel to process image files is a strong steganographic payload extraction indicator; correlate with rule 104026 (tax installer parent) on the same host (T1027.003)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1027.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>dcrat,dragonreturn,defense_evasion,steganography,bitmap_payload,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104028: DcRAT CreateRemoteThread injection into svchost.exe
       Sysmon EventID 8 (CreateRemoteThread) - Wazuh SID 61644 + eventID=8
       MITRE: T1055 (Process Injection)

       After decrypting and loading the .NET assembly in memory (bypassing
       AMSI), the DcRAT loader injects the payload into svchost.exe using
       the classic VirtualAllocEx + WriteProcessMemory + CreateRemoteThread
       sequence. Targeting svchost.exe allows the injected DcRAT code to
       blend with legitimate Windows service host processes and evade
       endpoint detection tools that monitor unusual standalone processes.

       The injection chain:
         1. CreateToolhelp32Snapshot() enumerates running processes
         2. Locate an svchost.exe instance (multiple exist in normal Windows)
         3. OpenProcess() with PROCESS_ALL_ACCESS to obtain a full handle
         4. VirtualAllocEx() allocates executable memory in svchost's space
         5. WriteProcessMemory() copies decrypted payload into allocated region
         6. CreateRemoteThread() starts execution within svchost.exe

       This rule catches step 6 (CreateRemoteThread from non-system source
       into svchost.exe). The sourceImage negate excludes known-legitimate
       Windows system binaries that legitimately create threads in svchost.

       NOTE: Sysmon EventID 8 logging must be explicitly enabled in the
       Sysmon configuration. Add <CreateRemoteThread onmatch="exclude"/>
       with exclusions for known-good system processes to reduce noise.

       False positives: security products (EDRs, AV engines) and some
       Windows system components do inject threads into svchost.exe.
       The negate pattern excludes the most common legitimate sources
       from System32/SysWOW64. Tune by adding confirmed-benign source
       paths (e.g., your EDR agent) to the negate list.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104028" level="14">
    <if_sid>61644</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^8$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\svchost\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.sourceImage" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)^C:\\Windows\\(System32|SysWOW64|WinSxS|Microsoft\.NET)\\|^C:\\Program Files\\Windows Defender\\</field>
    <description>DcRAT/DragonReturn: CreateRemoteThread injection into svchost.exe from non-system process $(win.eventdata.sourceImage) on $(win.eventdata.computer) - Operation DragonReturn DcRAT payload uses VirtualAllocEx + WriteProcessMemory + CreateRemoteThread to inject into svchost.exe for defense evasion; non-Windows-system source process creating remote thread in svchost.exe is near-certain process injection; isolate host immediately (T1055)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1055</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>dcrat,dragonreturn,defense_evasion,process_injection,svchost_injection,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104029: MixedSvc Windows service persistence (DcRAT)
       Windows System EventID 7045 (service install) - Wazuh SID 60103
       MITRE: T1543.003 (Create/Modify System Process: Windows Service)
              T1036.005 (Masquerading: Match Legitimate Name or Location)

       Operation DragonReturn establishes DcRAT persistence by installing
       a Windows service named "MixedSvc" with the display name "Windows
       Mixed Reality Service". This mimics the legitimate Windows Mixed
       Reality Portal service (MixedRealityPortal.exe in C:\Windows\System32)
       to blend into the service list and avoid casual administrator
       inspection.

       The key differentiator: the legitimate Windows Mixed Reality Portal
       service binary lives exclusively in C:\Windows\System32\ or
       C:\Program Files\WindowsApps\MicrosoftCorporationII.MixedReality*\.
       The DcRAT MixedSvc service binary runs from a non-Microsoft path
       (user AppData, ProgramData, TEMP, or a disguised install path).

       This rule triggers when:
         - A new service named MixedSvc (or matching "Mixed Reality" variants)
           is installed (EventID 7045)
         - AND the service binary is NOT from a known-legitimate Microsoft path

       False positives: virtually none. The legitimate Windows Mixed Reality
       service is pre-installed by Windows Update; EventID 7045 is not
       generated for pre-existing services. A fresh install from a non-system
       path matching this service name pattern is a confirmed DcRAT indicator.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104029" level="14">
    <if_sid>60103</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^7045$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.serviceName" type="pcre2">(?i)(MixedSvc|windows.*mixed.*reality|mixed.*reality.*serv)</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.imagePath" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(C:\\Windows\\System32\\MixedReality|C:\\Program Files\\WindowsApps\\MicrosoftCorporation.*MixedReality)</field>
    <description>DcRAT/DragonReturn: Suspicious MixedSvc service installed on $(win.eventdata.computer) [binary: $(win.eventdata.imagePath)] - Operation DragonReturn registers DcRAT as Windows service 'MixedSvc' masquerading as 'Windows Mixed Reality Service'; service binary located outside legitimate Microsoft paths is a confirmed DcRAT persistence mechanism; investigate $(win.eventdata.imagePath) immediately (T1543.003, T1036.005)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1543.003</id>
      <id>T1036.005</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>dcrat,dragonreturn,persistence,service_persistence,masquerading,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104030: DcRAT AMSI bypass via .NET reflection in PowerShell
       Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1562.001 (Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify Tools)

       Before loading the decrypted DcRAT .NET assembly in-memory,
       Operation DragonReturn's PowerShell stage patches the Windows
       Antimalware Scan Interface (AMSI) to prevent detection of the
       in-memory payload. The AMSI bypass technique patches the
       AmsiScanBuffer function in amsi.dll to always return AMSI_RESULT_CLEAN,
       enabling the DcRAT .NET assembly to be loaded via
       [System.Reflection.Assembly]::Load() without triggering AV scans.

       Common AMSI bypass patterns observed in this and related campaigns:
         - Direct reference to "AmsiScanBuffer" for function pointer resolution
         - Reference to "amsi.dll" for GetProcAddress-style resolution
         - Reference to System.Management.Automation.AmsiUtils (PowerShell's
           internal class that wraps AMSI; patching its methods disables AMSI)
         - Use of [System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::GetDelegateForFunctionPointer
           combined with amsi-related strings to patch the function in-place

       PowerShell commands containing AMSI-related strings are virtually
       never produced by legitimate PowerShell scripts or IT automation.
       This is a high-confidence indicator requiring immediate investigation.

       False positives: security awareness training tools that demonstrate
       AMSI bypass techniques in a controlled lab. Tune by suppressing
       known security lab hostnames.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104030" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(powershell|pwsh)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(AmsiScanBuffer|AmsiInitialize|amsi\.dll|System\.Management\.Automation\.AmsiUtils|GetDelegateForFunctionPointer.*amsi|Reflection\.Assembly\]::Load\(|UnsafeNativeMethods.*GetMethod.*['""]Amsi|Set-MpPreference.*DisableRealtimeMonitoring)</field>
    <description>DcRAT/DragonReturn: AMSI bypass via .NET reflection in PowerShell on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)] - Operation DragonReturn patches AmsiScanBuffer via .NET reflection to load the DcRAT .NET assembly without antivirus detection; PowerShell referencing AmsiScanBuffer, AmsiUtils, or combined GetDelegateForFunctionPointer+amsi patterns is a near-certain AMSI bypass; correlate with rule 104026 (tax installer) on the same host (T1562.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1562.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>dcrat,dragonreturn,defense_evasion,amsi_bypass,fileless_execution,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104031: DcRAT C2 connection to Operation DragonReturn IOCs
       Network/firewall logs - Wazuh SID 103000
       MITRE: T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols)

       Operation DragonReturn runs two parallel C2 channels after successful
       DcRAT deployment:

       1. DcRAT primary C2 (encrypted TLS):
          IP: 223.26.63.40, Port: 2671
          Infrastructure: ChinaNet ASN; Chinese-language web management
          panel exposed publicly at the time of discovery; attributed to
          the DcRAT builder's administrative interface.

       2. Gh0st RAT variant C2:
          Domain: kkxqbh[.]top, Port: 6666
          This component provides screen capture, file exfiltration,
          and reverse shell capabilities via the Gh0st RAT protocol.

       3. AsyncRAT/Quasar-family C2:
          Domain: ouewop[.]com, Port: 6351
          Second-stage implant providing additional persistence and
          redundant C2 access.

       4. Supplemental C2/staging:
          Domain: 1kkkkddd[.]com
          Used for payload staging and redundant C2 routing.

       All four IOCs are attributed to Operation DragonReturn by Seqrite
       as of 2026-06-17 with all submissions confirmed from India. Any
       connection to these IOCs from a monitored host indicates a completed
       DcRAT infection. Isolate the host and begin incident response.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104031" level="14">
    <if_sid>103000</if_sid>
    <match>223.26.63.40|kkxqbh\.top|ouewop\.com|1kkkkddd\.com</match>
    <description>DcRAT/DragonReturn: connection to confirmed Operation DragonReturn C2 infrastructure from $(agent.name) - DcRAT TLS C2 at 223.26.63.40:2671 (ChinaNet ASN; Chinese-language management panel) or Gh0st RAT domain kkxqbh.top:6666 or AsyncRAT domain ouewop.com:6351 or staging domain 1kkkkddd.com; China-nexus espionage targeting Indian tax professionals; isolate host and initiate IR immediately (T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>dcrat,dragonreturn,ioc_match,ioc_c2,china_nexus,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104032: DcRAT sustained C2 beaconing - active RAT confirmed
       Frequency rule on 104031 - 2 events in 120 seconds, same source
       MITRE: T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols)

       DcRAT beacons to its C2 at regular intervals (configurable in the
       DcRAT builder; default is every 10-60 seconds). A single match on
       rule 104031 could theoretically be a transient false positive (e.g.,
       cached DNS, miscategorized URL), but two or more connections from the
       same source host to Operation DragonReturn C2 infrastructure within
       120 seconds is unambiguous evidence of an active, running DcRAT
       implant performing regular C2 check-ins.

       This rule confirms persistent beaconing and should trigger CRITICAL
       response: immediate host isolation, memory forensic acquisition,
       and escalation to incident response. Given the China-nexus attribution
       and espionage motivation, sensitive documents and credentials on the
       host should be treated as compromised.

       Action: Isolate host from network. Capture volatile memory before
       powering off. Preserve Sysmon and Windows Event logs. File an
       incident and notify Security Operations Manager.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104032" level="15" frequency="2" timeframe="120">
    <if_matched_sid>104031</if_matched_sid>
    <same_source_ip />
    <description>DcRAT/DragonReturn: ACTIVE RAT BEACONING CONFIRMED from $(agent.name) - 2+ connections to Operation DragonReturn C2 infrastructure (223.26.63.40 / kkxqbh.top / ouewop.com / 1kkkkddd.com) within 120 seconds; DcRAT implant is running and performing regular C2 check-ins; China-nexus espionage against Indian tax sector; CRITICAL: isolate host NOW, capture memory, escalate to IR (T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>dcrat,dragonreturn,ioc_c2,beaconing,active_rat,china_nexus,critical,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
